WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.068 --> 00:00:02.610
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome to Cloud
Security Basics, a new series

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.890
where we explain the
ins and outs of securing

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.930
your application
on Google Cloud.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:07.490
Sound fun?

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.490
Then stick around,
because in this episode,

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.060
you'll learn about the third of
three distinct areas of cloud

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.408
security risk.

00:00:13.408 --> 00:00:13.950
The platform.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.401
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.700
[PHONE DIALS]

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.530
BRAD ACTOR: Hello,
Cloud detective.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.000
You may have understood
securing access and data,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.970
but what about the platform?

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.980
What makes the physical
and virtual hardware

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.602
Google provides secure?

00:00:43.602 --> 00:00:45.060
Once your data is
in the cloud, how

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.700
do you know it's really safe?

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.220
What if VMs aren't
being properly isolated

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.190
from other workloads, leading
to a tricky tenants isolation

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.910
exploit?

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.570
Are the physical devices being
properly secured and checked

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.170
for malware?

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.410
What about people at Google
looking at your data?

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:06.090
What if one of my hench people
gets into a Google data center?

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.780
For your sake, I hope you know
the answer to my questions.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.940
CARTER MORGAN: OK,
here's what we know.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.820
Platform represents the physical
systems that house and deliver

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.210
information.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.660
It's important to understand
the abstract notion of a user

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.090
accessing information is
realized in physical systems,

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.320
and the sum total of those
systems is the platform.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.840
We often think of the platform
as a server side thing

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.630
but really, it's everything
from the user's fingertips,

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.850
to the bit on the
disk, the in between,

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.700
the server which runs
applications, and so on.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.567
But since we have a
shared security model,

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:39.150
it's important to
remember that Google

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.890
is responsible for
ensuring the physical

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.480
and virtual hardware
Google provides

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.160
is operating as it should.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.200
Luckily, Google
Cloud Services are

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.120
designed to deliver
better security

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.300
than many traditional
on-premise solutions.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.220
Because Google runs on
the same infrastructure

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.900
they make available
to their customers,

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.670
your organization
directly benefits

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.323
from these protections.

00:01:58.323 --> 00:02:00.490
Google uses a multilayered
defense in depth approach

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:01.580
to security.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.040
Each layer, from physical
data center components

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.340
to hardware provenance, secure
boot, secure interservice

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.039
communication, secure
data, and protected access

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:11.660
to services from the internet--

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.280
each of these has
strict controls

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.490
for access and privilege
and is continually evolving.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.470
Let's dive deeper into this.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.710
So how does Google
Cloud really protect

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.600
the physical and virtual
hardware provides?

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.780
Well, check this out.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.590
As part of their ongoing
efforts to secure the Cloud,

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.540
Google Cloud has 850-plus
security engineers,

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:38.340
$200 billion annual security
investment, a 24/7 active

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.640
watch, 160-plus
academic research

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.040
papers on security,
a bug bounty program,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.520
a penetration testing program.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.800
This shows the level
of investment Google

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.450
puts into security.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.120
Google security teams triage,
investigate, and respond

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.900
to incidents 24 hours
a day, 365 days a year,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.610
and conduct regular
exercises to measure

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.580
and improve security
detection and response.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.860
On the application
side, Google Cloud

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.613
provides libraries
and frameworks

00:03:06.613 --> 00:03:08.280
that prevent developers
from introducing

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.460
certain classes of security
bugs like cross-site scripting

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.260
vulnerabilities and web apps.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.920
On Google Cloud, automated tools
like fuzzers, static analysis

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.810
tools, and web
security scanners are

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.150
used to detect security bugs.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.910
On the access side, Google
makes heavy investment

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.010
in protecting Google's
employees' devices

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.740
and credentials from
compromise, using technologies

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.920
and strict policies for physical
computer data and network

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.820
security, access management,
security login, and so much

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:36.533
more.

00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:38.760
Ah, that's it.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.440
I know the answer
to Brad's question.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.390
BRAD ACTOR: Hello, detective.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.720
I see you've been quite busy
and solved my little question.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.510
What's really keeping your data
safe once it's in the cloud?

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.340
Ah, and you found
the key concept.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.270
Clients secure
their applications,

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:59.630
and Google secures
the infrastructure.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:02.840
The platform is secure, because
that's Google's responsibility.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.010
And security is
foundational to everything

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.760
Google Cloud does, from
designing data centers,

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.940
to software, to processes.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.580
But most importantly,
because Google Cloud

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.620
runs on the very same
infrastructure it provides

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.899
to customers, all
of those protections

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:21.529
can be used by people like
you to protect your business.

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:23.780
Very good, very good.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.160
You've answered
today's question,

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.590
but be ready because
next time, I'm

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.660
going to make you dig
deeper into the platform

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.720
to see exactly how Google
Cloud protects the user's data.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.220
CARTER MORGAN: So
there you have it.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.790
Another episode of
Cloud Security basics.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.670
Next episode, we'll focus on how
Google Cloud protects the data

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:42.170
that lives on their servers.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.780
In the meantime, if you want
to take a deeper dive into how

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:46.520
Google secures
its platform, then

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.728
check out the article LinkedIn
the description below.

00:04:48.728 --> 00:04:51.312
And stay tuned for the rest of
the Cloud Security Basic Series

00:04:51.312 --> 00:04:52.880
because when it
comes to security,

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.740
you can't let bad actors win.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

